𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your application needs database credentials in Kubernetes. How will you store and manage them securely?”

In production, I would never hardcode passwords, API keys, or tokens directly into application manifests or container images.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Create a Kubernetes Secret

kubectl create secret generic db-secret \
  --from-literal=DB_USER=appuser \
  --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD='<password>' \
  -n <namespace>

Use placeholders in examples and never expose real credentials in shell history or shared terminals.

2️⃣ Verify the Secret

kubectl get secrets -n <namespace>
kubectl describe secret db-secret -n <namespace>

Avoid printing Secret values unnecessarily.

3️⃣ Inject secrets into the application

Reference the Secret through environment variables or mount it as a file, depending on the application's requirements.

4️⃣ Restrict access using RBAC

Grant only the required users and ServiceAccounts permission to access Secrets in the appropriate namespace.

5️⃣ Enable stronger protection

Kubernetes Secrets are base64-encoded, not automatically encrypted at rest in every configuration.

For production, enable encryption at rest and consider an external secrets manager such as a cloud secrets service or Vault.

6️⃣ Rotate and audit credentials

Establish a rotation process, review access permissions, and ensure sensitive values are not exposed in logs or CI/CD output.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Storing a password in a Kubernetes Secret is only one part of securing it.

Access control, encryption, rotation, auditing, and safe application handling are equally important.
